// pfodWeb NOTE: excludes GPIO5/6/7/10 - dedicated onboard LoRa radio SPI
// bus (LORA_SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK). Excludes GPIO40/41/42 - LoRa BUSY/RST/
// ANTPWR control lines. Excludes GPIO4 - LoRa DIO1. Excludes
// GPIO14/15/16 - dedicated onboard TFT display control lines (TFT_DC/
// RST/BL, never aliased elsewhere). The general SPI bus (SS=17/MOSI=11/
// MISO=13/SCK=12) stays available - TFT_CS reuses the same GPIO17 as SS,
// suggesting the vendor intends this bus to double as general-purpose.
//
// pfodWeb NOTE (button/LED tagging pass, 2026-07-12): this board.json
// already had the dedicated-hardware exclusions above from an earlier
// pass, but never had button/LED tagging applied. Applied now, board
// identified as DFRobot SKU DFR1195 "LoRaWAN ESP32-S3 Dev Board" (SX1262
// LoRa radio, 0.96" LCD, physical buttons - matches this file's LORA_*/
// TFT_* defines and LED_BUILTIN=21 exactly):
//  - GPIO21 (LED_BUILTIN) tagged "led_high" - vendor wiki
//    (wiki.dfrobot.com/dfr1195/) confirms "LED light, connected to
//    GPIO21" as a plain LED (not NeoPixel; no separate NEOPIXEL/RGB
//    define exists in this file either). Polarity not stated explicitly
//    by the vendor (no "active low"/"active high" wording found on the
//    wiki or in the official getting-started Blink example at
//    wiki.dfrobot.com/dfr1195/docs/22839, which is the plain
//    pinMode(led,OUTPUT)/digitalWrite(led,HIGH)/delay/digitalWrite(led,LOW)
//    pattern with no polarity comment). Tagged led_high on the strength
//    of (a) the vendor's own tutorial demonstrating HIGH as the first/
//    "on" state for this exact GPIO on this exact board, and (b) ESP32/
//    ESP32-S3 dev-board onboard LEDs are conventionally wired active-high
//    (anode-to-GPIO through resistor to GND), unlike the ESP8266 NodeMCU
//    convention - no contradicting source found. Flag for re-check if a
//    schematic surfaces later.
//  - GPIO18 (Key1) tagged "button" - vendor wiki confirms "Key1: Button
//    1, connected to GPIO18"; a second independent wiki summary states
//    "the main controller can be proactively woken up via a button
//    connected to IO18 to collect and send data" (wake-button use),
//    corroborating it as a genuine user-accessible pushbutton. GPIO18 has
//    no other role in this file (not LoRa/TFT/bus-aliased - see SDA=8/
//    SCL=9/SS=17/MOSI=11/MISO=13/SCK=12/TX=43/RX=44 above, none match).
//  - GPIO0 (Key2) tagged "button" - vendor wiki: "Key2: Button 2,
//    connected to GPIO0. Press this button and reset the device to enter
//    boot mode; it can be used as a normal button after the system
//    starts." Standard BOOT-strap-button topology, consistent with this
//    chip's own pinNotes "0": "Must be HIGH for normal boot" (unchanged).
//    Matches established precedent (e.g. waveshare_esp32_s3_touch_lcd_28)
//    of tagging a confirmed BOOT/user-dual-role button as "button".
//  - RST (hardware reset button) - not mapped to any GPIO per the vendor
//    wiki, not taggable, left alone.
//  - Charge indicator LED - vendor wiki describes a separate "charging
//    status indicator" (off/no power or full; steady on/charging) with
//    no GPIO assignment given - almost certainly wired directly to the
//    battery-charger IC's status pin, not an MCU GPIO. Not taggable, left
//    alone.
// See boardsDetails/esp32/esp32s3/dfrobot_lorawan_esp32s3/notes.txt for
// the full research trail and sources.
{
  "boardName": "ESP32S3",
